# Between which years did the largest increases in median wealth occur among black females?

**Interval label: 1985-1990** (primary, literal codebook reading; n = 1,548 black female respondents).
Median composite wealth: **$175 (1985) -> $2,600 (1990) -> $4,000 (1996)**.
Delta 1985->1990 = **+$2,425** ; delta 1990->1996 = **+$1,400**. Both are increases; the
1985-1990 interval is larger by **$1,025** in absolute median change. % change: +1,385.7% (1985-1990,
NOT meaningful - base median $175 is effectively zero) and +53.8% (1990-1996).

**The conclusion is NOT robust to how zeros are handled.** Treating the 30-40% of exact-zero wealth
values as technical missing gives medians $3,832 -> $8,100 -> $19,800 (deltas +$4,268 vs +$11,700) and
restricting to positive wealth gives $4,800 -> $11,057 -> $25,050 (deltas +$6,257 vs +$13,993). In
BOTH alternative branches the winner flips to **1990-1996**, where it is also the larger increase in
absolute and relative terms. Two of three defensible specifications therefore point at 1990-1996; the
primary branch points at 1985-1990 only because the 1985 median sits on the edge of a block of zeros
(47.5% of black females are at or below $0 in 1985), so its small $175 level and hence its large jump
are an artifact of zero-coding rather than evidence of an exceptionally strong 1985-1990 gain.

Robustness within a branch: one bootstrap (B=2,000, seed 20261023, resampling respondents, primary
branch) puts P(1985-1990 has the larger increase) = 96.6% - i.e. sampling noise is not what drives the
disagreement; the zero-coding choice is. No weights or variance estimates exist in the file, so all
comparisons are point estimates without confidence intervals.
